{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgzBpX6XhPfhNgyxtnDog","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It's not dead code, but nothing records why the tests are run with it.\n\n**What it does:** `kiln/timeparse.py:12` reads it. With `KILN_LEGACY_TZ=1`, `parse()` treats timestamps that have no timezone as UTC, which is how kiln worked before 2.0. Without it, those timestamps are read as local time.\n\n**Why step 4 sets it:** neither SETUP.md nor git history says. The variable came in with the initial commit `d274cef` along with everything else, so there's no message explaining it. My guess is that some tests or fixtures assume naive timestamps are UTC. Setting the variable makes their results the same on every machine, whatever its local timezone.\n\n**Whether it's still needed:** the tests also pass without it here (3 run, all pass). That doesn't settle it. If this machine's clock is set to UTC, both modes give the same results, so a test that depends on the variable could still pass. A better check is to run the tests without the variable under a timezone other than UTC, e.g. `TZ=Asia/Tokyo python3 -m unittest`. If they still pass, and nothing else relies on legacy parsing, it's probably safe to drop.\n\nI haven't changed step 4. If you know the reason, or want me to run that check, tell me and I'll update or remove it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":544,"cache_read_input_tokens":22228,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":544},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<uuid-1>","uuid":"<uuid-41>","timestamp":"2026-11-13T03:05:47.205Z","request_id":"req_011CgzBpWngXtctB8uq855KQ"}
